"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[27398],{427398:e=>{e.exports="# Tableau Metadata Extraction\n\nThe Tableau workbook is exposed as structured workbook metadata. This is the **default** channel for any Phase A / Phase B question that touches datasources, calculations, parameters, dashboards, or worksheets. Use the raw `.twb` XML (`xml_extraction.md`) only as a fallback for attributes the metadata does not surface.\n\n## Available entities\n\n| Entity              | Read it for                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `overview`          | Workbook gist — `entity_counts` for every entity, `calculations_overview` (totals, LOD presence + type counts, table-calc / nested / parameter flags, max nesting depth), `filters_overview` (totals, datasource vs worksheet filter counts). Cheap orientation check before paginating large entities. |\n| `calculations`      | Formulas, LOD / table-calc flags, dependencies, parameter references. Drives the skip-criteria decision tree (`SKILL.md`) and SQL translation (`calc_to_sql.md`).                                                                                                                                       |\n| `parameters`        | Name + caption + domain. Not migratable — enumerate so calc / filter dependencies on parameters can be flagged as skipped.                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `datasources`       | Connections, columns, relations, joins. Drives metric-view `source:` SQL and the column-to-table mapping.                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `worksheets`        | Chart type, shelves, encodings, filters, datasources used. Drives visual-type selection (`visuals.md`) and per-widget filter wiring.                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `dashboards`        | Zones, dashboard-level filters, actions. Drives page layout and global-filter detection (`filters.md`).                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `extraction_errors` | Parse / extraction errors recorded by the extractor. Read when troubleshooting unexpected behaviour.                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n\n## Reading idioms\n\nEach entity supports filtering by record fields, projecting a subset of fields, and paginating large arrays (max 200 records per page). Array responses include `total_count` (count after `where`); advance `offset` until `offset + data.length` reaches `total_count`.\n\nGeneralised examples:\n\n- All calculation names → entity `calculations`, fields `['name']`.\n- Just LOD calcs → entity `calculations`, where `is_lod_expression` truthy.\n- One specific record → entity `calculations`, where `name` equals `[Sales]`.\n- Two-level nested filter (e.g. relations of a specific datasource) → fetch the datasource by caption, then walk its `relations[]` client-side rather than chaining filters.\n- Long entity → page through with `offset: 0, limit: 50`, then advance `offset` while more records remain per `total_count`.\n\nFor broad queries, check `entity_counts` in the overview before fetching values; narrow with a filter when the count is large.\n\n## Schemas\n\n### `calculations[]`\n\nA calculated field on a datasource.\n\n- `name` — bracketed identifier (e.g. `[Sales Amount]`).\n- `caption` — display label.\n- `formula` — Tableau expression text (often multi-line).\n- `data_type` — `string` | `integer` | `real` | `date` | `datetime` | `boolean`.\n- `calculation_class` — `tableau` for native; otherwise external (e.g. `python`, `r`).\n- `role` — `dimension` | `measure`.\n- `aggregation` — pre-applied aggregation if any; null otherwise.\n- `is_lod_expression` — true for `{FIXED|INCLUDE|EXCLUDE …}` formulas.\n- `lod_type` — `FIXED` | `INCLUDE` | `EXCLUDE` | null.\n- `is_table_calculation` — true if the formula uses any table-calc function.\n- `table_calc_functions` — array of function names used (e.g. `[\"RUNNING_SUM\"]`).\n- `dependencies` — array of bracketed identifiers referenced in the formula (brackets stripped).\n- `parameter_references` — array of parameter names referenced (without `[Parameters].` prefix).\n\nThe `dependencies`, `parameter_references`, `lod_type`, and `table_calc_functions` fields let you apply the `SKILL.md` skip-criteria decision tree without parsing the formula text.\n\n### `parameters[]`\n\nA workbook parameter.\n\n- `name` — bracketed identifier.\n- `caption` — display label.\n- `data_type` — same enum as columns.\n- `current_value` — current selection (string).\n- `domain_type` — `list` | `range` | `all`.\n- `allowable_values` — array of allowed values (when `domain_type=list`).\n- `range_min`, `range_max`, `range_granularity` — populated when `domain_type=range`.\n- `default_format` — formatting hint, often null.\n\n### `datasources[]`\n\nA Tableau data source.\n\n- `name` — internal id (often `federated.*`).\n- `caption` — display name.\n- `version` — Tableau version that authored the datasource.\n- `has_connection` — false for parameter-only inline datasources.\n- `is_inline` — true for inline (workbook-embedded) datasources.\n- `connections` — array of `connection` objects (see below).\n- `columns` — array of `column` objects (see below).\n- `relations` — array of `relation` objects forming the physical layer.\n- `joins` — array of explicit joins between relations.\n- `column_table_map` — `{ column_name: relation_name }` mapping each column to its parent relation.\n\nNested shapes:\n\n- `connection`: `name`, `connection_type` (e.g. `federated`, `sqlserver`, `databricks`, `hyper`), `server`, `database`, `schema_name`, `filename`, `custom_sql`, `properties`.\n- `column`: `name` (bracketed), `caption`, `data_type`, `role`, `aggregation`, `default_format`, `ordinal`, `is_hidden`, `is_calculated`, `calculation_formula` (set when `is_calculated`).\n- `relation`: `name`, `table` (e.g. `[catalog].[schema].[table]` — strip brackets, join with dots), `connection`, `type` (`table` | `join` | `text` | `query` | …), `columns`.\n\n### `worksheets[]`\n\nA Tableau worksheet (one chart).\n\n- `name` — internal name.\n- `title`, `caption` — display labels (and their `*_formatted` variants for rich text).\n- `chart_type`, `chart_subtype` — derived chart classification.\n- `mark_class` — Tableau-native mark (e.g. `Line`, `Bar`, `Circle`, `Pie`, `Map`, `Automatic`).\n- `columns_shelf`, `rows_shelf` — arrays of field names placed on each shelf.\n- `columns_shelf_detailed`, `rows_shelf_detailed` — same lists with `derivation` (Sum/Avg/Count/CountD/Min/Max/Year-Trunc/...) and `field_type` (`nominal` | `ordinal` | `quantitative`).\n- `mark_encoding` — object keyed by `color` | `size` | `shape` | `detail` | `text` | `tooltip` → array of field names on that encoding.\n- `mark_encoding_detailed` — same with derivation/type detail.\n- `filters` — array of `filter` objects (see below).\n- `datasources`, `datasource_captions` — internal ids and display names of datasources the worksheet pulls from.\n- `parameters_used` — array of parameter names referenced.\n- `annotations`, `actions`, `is_visible`, `custom_tooltip`, `custom_tooltip_formatted`, `measure_values_config`, `reference_line_labels`, `show_caption`, `table_calc_contexts` — auxiliary fields.\n\nWorksheet `filter`: `field`, `filter_type` (`categorical` | `quantitative` | `relative-date` | …), `values`, `include_null`, `is_global`, `is_context`.\n\n### `dashboards[]`\n\nA Tableau dashboard (one page in the published workbook).\n\n- `name` — display name.\n- `sizing_mode`, `width`, `height` — sizing hints (often null in modern workbooks).\n- `zones` — array of `zone` objects forming the layout.\n- `filters` — array of dashboard-level filter widgets.\n- `actions` — array of dashboard actions (highlight / filter / URL navigation).\n\nNested shapes:\n\n- `zone`: `zone_id`, `zone_type` (`worksheet` | `filter` | `text` | `container` | `legend` | …), `x`, `y`, `width`, `height`, `worksheet_name` (set when `zone_type=worksheet`), `orientation`, `text_content`, `formatted_text`, `param_value`. Coordinates are Tableau's internal grid — use them as ordering hints only; the dashboard sub-agent handles its own placement.\n- Dashboard `filter`: `filter_type`, `mode`, `filter_class`, `column_type`, `datasource_id`, `field_name`, `current_values`, `default_values`, `min_value`, `max_value`, `period_type`, `first_period`, `is_global`, plus geometry (`x`, `y`, `width`, `height`).\n\n## Derived facts the metadata doesn't surface directly\n\n- **Hyper extract rename.** If `relations[i].table` starts with `[Extract]` and `relations[i].name` matches `Foo (catalog.schema.foo)_HASH`, the parenthetical between `(` and `)` is the real `catalog.schema.table`; the prefix before `(` is the clean table name. Apply the rename consistently when referencing the relation in `joins[]` and `column_table_map`.\n- **Parameter references on a calculation.** A calculation references a parameter when its `dependencies[]` contains an entry starting with `Parameters.`. Strip that prefix to get the parameter name. The `parameter_references` field is the same list pre-stripped when present.\n- **Federated connection wrappers.** A datasource's `connections[]` may include a `connection_type: 'federated'` wrapper around the real connection — skip the federated entry. If `database` is null on the underlying connection, look for it in `properties.dbname` or `properties.catalog` (the key varies by connector — Snowflake / Databricks / Hive).\n\n## Decision Tree: JSON or XML?\n\n1. **Name / type / role / structure / formula text** → JSON.\n2. **Attribute on a specific XML element that the metadata does not surface** (raw style-rule formats, version-specific quirks, datasource-level filter expressions) → XML (`xml_extraction.md`).\n3. **Unsure if a field exists in the metadata** → try the metadata first; an empty result on a known entity means the workbook has none — then fall back to XML.\n"}}]);
